    {
      "data": {
        "label": "AI Chat Node",
        "model": "gemini-3-pro",
        "prompt1": "You are given the COMPLETE subtitle script in SRT format or plain text containing subtitle segments.\n\nYour task is to generate EXACTLY ONE realistic VIDEO GENERATION PROMPT for EVERY subtitle segment.\n\nThe number of generated video prompts MUST be EXACTLY EQUAL to the number of subtitle segments.\n\nHIGHEST PRIORITY RULE — STRICT 1:1 MAPPING:\n\nONE subtitle segment = EXACTLY ONE video prompt.\n\nNEVER combine subtitle segments.\nNEVER skip a subtitle segment.\nNEVER generate multiple prompts for one subtitle segment.\nNEVER generate extra prompts.\nNEVER merge segments even when they describe the same scene, character, action, location or event.\nEven if consecutive subtitles describe one continuous action or scene, each subtitle MUST receive its own separate prompt.\nEven if a subtitle contains only a few words, it MUST receive exactly one complete prompt.\n\nThe final number of prompts MUST equal the total number of subtitle segments.\n\nSTEP 1 — READ AND COUNT THE COMPLETE SCRIPT\n\nRead the ENTIRE subtitle script from beginning to end BEFORE generating any prompt.\n\nFor SRT:\n\nEach numbered subtitle entry = ONE subtitle segment.\n\nMultiple dialogue lines inside one numbered entry still count as ONE segment.\n\nInternally create a strict mapping:\n\nSubtitle 1 → Prompt 1\nSubtitle 2 → Prompt 2\nSubtitle 3 → Prompt 3\n...\nSubtitle N → Prompt N\n\nDo NOT output this mapping.\n\nBefore generating the final answer, internally verify the total number of subtitle segments.\n\nSTEP 2 — UNDERSTAND THE COMPLETE SCRIPT\n\nBefore generating prompts, understand the entire script and internally establish:\n\n* Characters and recurring characters\n* Character appearance, age, gender, hairstyle, face, clothing and body characteristics\n* Locations and environments\n* Objects and their positions\n* Time of day\n* Weather\n* Lighting\n* Actions and events\n* Spatial relationships\n* Temporal progression\n* Visual style\n* Continuity between scenes\n\nRecurring characters MUST maintain the same established appearance, clothing and relevant physical characteristics unless the subtitle explicitly indicates a change.\n\nMaintain environmental, spatial, temporal, lighting and visual continuity whenever appropriate.\n\nSTEP 3 — GENERATE EXACTLY ONE PROMPT FOR EACH SUBTITLE\n\nProcess every subtitle segment individually according to the original subtitle order.\n\nFor EACH subtitle segment, generate EXACTLY ONE realistic VIDEO GENERATION PROMPT representing the specific visual meaning and physical action of THAT subtitle.\n\nDo NOT generate a prompt from a general interpretation of the entire script.\n\nEach prompt MUST represent its corresponding subtitle segment.\n\nThe prompts MUST remain separate even when segments belong to the same continuous scene.\n\nSTEP 4 — MANDATORY FIVE-COMPONENT STRUCTURE\n\nEVERY generated video prompt MUST contain ALL FIVE components:\n\n1. CINEMATOGRAPHY\n   Shot type, framing, camera angle, camera position, camera behavior, camera movement, lens or focus when relevant.\n\n2. SUBJECT\n   The primary character, object, animal or vehicle, including relevant established visual characteristics.\n\n3. ACTION\n   The specific physical action represented by THAT subtitle segment.\n\nThe action MUST directly represent the subtitle meaning.\n\nDo NOT invent unnecessary actions or replace the subtitle's primary action with unrelated cinematic action.\n\n4. CONTEXT\n   Relevant location, environment, time, weather, objects and spatial circumstances.\n\n5. STYLE & AMBIANCE\n   Visual style, realism, lighting, color, atmosphere, mood and relevant natural or diegetic sound.\n\nALL FIVE components are mandatory for EVERY prompt.\n\nThey MUST be naturally integrated into one professional Veo 3.1 video prompt.\n\nDo NOT output the component names as labels.\n\nDo NOT output the five components as separate sections.\n\nSTEP 5 — VIDEO MOTION AND PHYSICAL CONTINUITY\n\nEvery prompt must describe a REAL EVENT UNFOLDING THROUGH TIME, not a static image.\n\nAll physical movement must be continuous and physically plausible.\n\nCharacters, objects and body parts MUST move through physically connected states.\n\nNEVER allow:\n\n* Teleportation\n* Sudden unexplained appearance or disappearance\n* Sudden position changes\n* Impossible body movement\n* Extra, missing or disconnected limbs\n* Objects changing position without physical interaction\n* Objects passing through solid surfaces\n* Impossible collisions or interactions\n* Instant posture or orientation changes\n* Unexplained spatial discontinuity\n\nActions must follow logical temporal order and realistic cause and effect.\n\nWhen a character interacts with an object, maintain believable:\n\napproach → contact → interaction → movement → release\n\nSTEP 6 — CAMERA CONTINUITY\n\nThe camera must behave like a real physical camera.\n\nCamera movement may include:\n\n* Locked-off\n* Slow tracking\n* Slow dolly\n* Lateral tracking\n* Subject tracking\n* Slow pan\n* Slow tilt\n* Subtle push-in\n* Subtle pull-out\n* Gentle handheld movement\n\nCamera movement MUST remain continuous and physically plausible.\n\nDo NOT suddenly change camera position, height, direction, distance, perspective or viewing angle.\n\nDo NOT introduce unexplained cuts or instant perspective changes unless explicitly required by the subtitle.\n\nSTEP 7 — ENVIRONMENT AND OBJECT CONTINUITY\n\nObjects and environmental elements must remain consistent throughout each shot.\n\nDo NOT randomly introduce, remove or reposition objects.\n\nEnvironmental movement must have a believable physical cause such as wind, gravity, water, weather, machinery or human activity.\n\nMaintain consistent spatial relationships between characters, objects and the environment.\n\nSTEP 8 — MOTION COMPLEXITY CONTROL\n\nKeep the physical action as simple as possible while accurately representing the subtitle.\n\nPrioritize:\n\nSUBTITLE MEANING\n→ PRIMARY ACTION\n→ CONTINUOUS MOVEMENT\n→ PHYSICAL INTERACTION\n→ SPATIAL CONTINUITY\n→ TEMPORAL CONTINUITY\n→ CAMERA CONTINUITY\n→ REALISTIC PHYSICS\n\nDo NOT invent unnecessary actions, movements, objects, interactions or choreography.\n\nA simple physically coherent action is preferable to unnecessary complexity.\n\nSTEP 9 — CONTINUITY BETWEEN SUBTITLES\n\nEach subtitle produces an independent prompt.\n\nHowever, prompts belonging to the same continuous scene MUST maintain appropriate continuity of:\n\n* Character identity and appearance\n* Clothing\n* Location\n* Environment\n* Objects\n* Spatial relationships\n* Time of day\n* Lighting\n* Weather\n* Visual style\n\nContinuity MUST NEVER cause subtitles to be merged.\n\nSTEP 10 — SHORT OR ABSTRACT SUBTITLES\n\nA short subtitle MUST still produce exactly ONE complete video prompt.\n\nIf the subtitle describes an abstract concept, represent its meaning through realistic visible actions, human behavior, environments or physical situations.\n\nDo NOT create subtitles, captions, written words, diagrams, symbols or interface elements unless the subtitle explicitly requires visible text.\n\nSTEP 11 — FINAL INTERNAL VALIDATION\n\nBefore producing the final answer, silently verify ALL of the following:\n\n1. Total subtitle segments = total video prompts.\n2. Every subtitle has exactly one prompt.\n3. No subtitle was skipped.\n4. No subtitles were merged.\n5. No extra prompts were created.\n6. Prompt order exactly matches subtitle order.\n7. Every prompt represents its specific subtitle.\n8. Every prompt contains CINEMATOGRAPHY.\n9. Every prompt contains SUBJECT.\n10. Every prompt contains ACTION.\n11. Every prompt contains CONTEXT.\n12. Every prompt contains STYLE & AMBIANCE.\n13. Every prompt describes video motion, not a static image.\n14. Physical movement is continuous and plausible.\n15. Characters and objects maintain spatial continuity.\n16. Camera movement is continuous and plausible.\n17. No unnecessary actions or objects were invented.\n18. Continuity between subtitle segments is preserved.\n\nIf ANY validation condition fails, silently correct the prompts before producing the final answer.\n\nThe 1:1 mapping rule has absolute priority.\n\nSTEP 12 — OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nOutput ONLY the generated video prompts.\n\nExactly ONE prompt for EACH subtitle segment.\n\nEach prompt MUST be on its own line.\n\nSeparate consecutive prompts with exactly ONE blank line.\n\nDo NOT include:\n\n* Bullet points\n* Subtitle text\n* Segment numbers\n* Labels\n* Headings\n* Explanations\n* Commentary\n* Counts\n* Introductory text\n* Closing text\n\nDo NOT output:\n\nCINEMATOGRAPHY:\nSUBJECT:\nACTION:\nCONTEXT:\nSTYLE & AMBIANCE:\n\nThese are internal structural requirements only.\n\nThe final output MUST contain EXACTLY the same number of video prompts as subtitle segments.\n",
        "prompt2": "Đây là Chat Văn bản, không phải tạo ảnh. Prompt phải đảm bảo không vi phạm chính sách labs.google/fx/tools/flow .  Mỗi Prompt phải viết trên 1 dòng, có Số thứ tự ở đầu. Viết prompt trực tiếp, không giải thích, không ghi chú. ",
        "resultMode": "multi"
      },
